Subject: [PATCH 5/5] Core/MIPS: make FakeJit concrete again

JitInterface declares

    virtual const u8 *GetCodeBase() const = 0;

and FakeJit does not implement it, so `new MIPSComp::FakeJit(mipsState)`
in CreateNativeJit() fails to compile: allocating an object of abstract
class type.

That call is the fallback arm of CreateNativeJit(), taken on every
architecture without a native JIT backend, so the only builds that reach
it are the ones nobody runs — which is why this has gone unnoticed.

nullptr is the honest answer and the same one IRJit gives: FakeJit never
emits code, so there is no code base for the debugger to display.

diff --git a/Core/MIPS/fake/FakeJit.h b/Core/MIPS/fake/FakeJit.h
index bab59c000..c6b8eb9ef 100644
--- a/Core/MIPS/fake/FakeJit.h
+++ b/Core/MIPS/fake/FakeJit.h
@@ -47,6 +47,11 @@ class FakeJit : public FakeGen::FakeXCodeBlock, public JitInterface, public MIPS
 	void Compile(u32 em_address) override;	// Compiles a block at current MIPS PC
 	void DoJit(u32 em_address, JitBlock *b);
 
+	// FakeJit never emits anything, so there is no code to point a debugger at. This is
+	// the same answer IRJit gives, and it is what makes the class concrete: JitInterface
+	// declares GetCodeBase() pure virtual, and CreateNativeJit() instantiates FakeJit on
+	// every architecture without a native backend.
+	const u8 *GetCodeBase() const override { return nullptr; }
 	const u8 *GetCrashHandler() const override { return nullptr; }
 	bool CodeInRange(const u8 *ptr) const override { return IsInSpace(ptr); }
 	bool DescribeCodePtr(const u8 *ptr, std::string &name) override;
